{
  "id": "SPEC-002",
  "purpose": "Give callisto consumers a working, documented mechanism that places CI-built native binary artifacts (napi-rs .node files, maturin wheels) into their platform-stub package directories before `callisto publish` runs, closing the gap between `callisto matrix`'s output and a working end-to-end release, with artifact names that stay unique when multiple packages in one workspace share a target triple.",
  "scope": "Two changes. (1) Rust: `crates/callisto-graph/src/matrix.rs`'s `artifact_name_for_triple(triple: &str) -> String` is replaced with `artifact_name_for_package_platform(package_name: &str, platform: &str, arch: &str, abi: Option<&str>) -> String`, returning `format!(\"{package_name}-{platform}-{arch}\")` when `abi` is `None`, or `format!(\"{package_name}-{platform}-{arch}-{abi}\")` when `abi` is `Some`. This matches napi-rs's own published-platform-package and recommended-CI-artifact-name convention (`<name>-<platform>-<arch>[-<abi>]`, e.g. `@scope/addon-darwin-arm64`, `@scope/addon-linux-x64-gnu`) rather than the raw Rust target triple. `build_platform_target` (already receiving `package_name` as a parameter) calls `triple_to_role(triple)` to destructure `(platform, arch, abi)` and passes those -- not the raw triple string -- into `artifact_name_for_package_platform`. This embeds the already-workspace-unique `package_name` (a value `PlatformTarget` already carries, sourced from each package's own identity) into `artifactName`, so two different packages declaring the same triple no longer produce colliding CI artifact names. (2) GitHub Actions: extends `.github/actions/callisto-action/action.yml`'s existing `callisto-orchestrate` composite-action step -- within the block already guarded by `[[ -n \"$INPUT_PUBLISH\" && \"$INPUT_PUBLISH\" != \"false\" ]]` (action.yml:167), immediately after `NATIVE_MATRIX` is computed, adds a placement loop that downloads each `nativeMatrix` entry's artifact by `artifactName` (pinned to the current run via `$GITHUB_RUN_ID`) and places its contents into `packageDir`. Also updates `.claude/specs/callisto-matrix.md` §6.2 and corrects `ARCHITECTURE.md` §12's broken example workflow.",
  "non_goals": [
    "Extending PlatformTargetKind to Java/JNI or .NET native-AOT targets -- a separate, later concern; this spec only routes the two kinds PlatformTargetKind already supports (Napi, Maturin).",
    "Prebuilt Rust CLI binaries distributed as GitHub Release assets (\"Tier 2\") -- a separate, related, deferred future spec.",
    "Homebrew tap formula generation/distribution -- deferred by the maintainer as a possible separate future tool.",
    "Go binary distribution -- already an existing non-goal per .claude/specs/callisto-matrix.md §8, not being revisited here.",
    "A generic callisto-build-action that dispatches the actual per-platform build (napi build --platform --release, etc.) -- callisto-matrix.md §7's secondary/optional proposal. This spec covers only placement of an already-built, correctly-named artifact; the build step itself is the consumer's own responsibility.",
    "Validating the content of a downloaded artifact (e.g. confirming a .node file is well-formed, or that it is actually a valid binary for its target triple). The placement step moves whatever files gh run download retrieves into packageDir; content correctness is the build job's responsibility and surfaces later, at publish or install time, not at placement.",
    "Adding a new required input to callisto-action to opt into placement. Placement is driven entirely by whether nativeMatrix is a non-empty array -- consumers with no native platform targets see zero behavior change.",
    "Implementing the nonexistent callisto publish-target Rust CLI subcommand referenced by ARCHITECTURE.md §12's status note (line 447). That subcommand is not needed for this requirement and is not built by this spec; the status note's reference to it (alongside Java/.NET native-AOT discovery) remains accurate and is deliberately left in place -- only the example workflow's own code block, which currently invokes the nonexistent subcommand directly and dereferences nonexistent matrix.os/matrix.package/matrix.target-as-scalar fields, is corrected.",
    "Guaranteeing that a binary placed at packageDir by this step is picked up and included in callisto publish's registry upload output (npm tarball, PyPI wheel, etc.). That round-trip guarantee is owned by whatever spec governs callisto publish's own native-binary packaging logic (crates/callisto-graph/src/commands/publish.rs) -- this spec's guarantee ends at the file being present on disk at packageDir before publish begins.",
    "Handling malformed or invalid-JSON nativeMatrix values within callisto-action's own in-step placement loop. NATIVE_MATRIX (action.yml:164) is produced and consumed entirely within one shell step under `set -o pipefail`, with `jq` already validating JSON syntax (and failing the step via `|| { echo \"::error::callisto matrix failed\"; exit 1; }`) before the placement loop added by this spec ever reads the value -- malformed JSON cannot reach this specific loop by construction. This does not apply to a consumer's own custom multi-job pipeline built against callisto-matrix.md §6.2's standalone reference snippet, where nativeMatrix genuinely crosses a job-output boundary; that snippet's own error handling is out of scope for this spec beyond the run-id pin AC-011 requires.",
    "Handling a missing (absent-key) or null artifactName/packageDir field on an otherwise-valid nativeMatrix entry. crates/callisto-model/src/matrix.rs's PlatformTarget declares both fields as plain `String` (not `Option<String>`), so serde can never serialize a missing or null value for either field into the JSON callisto matrix emits.",
    "Preserving the exact literal string format `native-{triple}` (the raw-triple format, with no package-name component, from before this spec's own change) or the intermediate two-segment `native-{package_name}-{triple}` format (a shape this spec never actually implemented) as a stable, versioned contract of callisto matrix's JSON output. This spec's actual implemented change makes artifactName equal to `{package_name}-{platform}-{arch}[-{abi}]` -- napi-rs's own published-package/CI-artifact-name convention, derived from `triple_to_role`'s platform/arch/abi decomposition rather than the raw triple -- to fix the collision defect while matching napi-rs tooling's own expectations. A consumer that hardcoded any prior format rather than treating artifactName as an opaque per-entry correlation token (its documented purpose) will observe a different string. No config override for this naming scheme is provided -- package_name is already sourced from the package's own identity, which is already user-configurable through the normal manifest name field; a separate artifact-naming override would duplicate that control for no benefit, since the artifact name is a CI-internal correlation id, never published or user-facing."
  ],
  "api_surface": [
    {
      "name": "artifact_name_for_package_platform",
      "signature": "pub(crate) fn artifact_name_for_package_platform(package_name: &str, platform: &str, arch: &str, abi: Option<&str>) -> String",
      "description": "Replaces `artifact_name_for_triple(triple: &str) -> String`. Returns `format!(\"{package_name}-{platform}-{arch}\")` when `abi` is `None`, or `format!(\"{package_name}-{platform}-{arch}-{abi}\")` when `abi` is `Some`, matching napi-rs's own published-platform-package and recommended-CI-artifact-name convention (`<name>-<platform>-<arch>[-<abi>]`). `build_platform_target` (crates/callisto-graph/src/matrix.rs:138-155), which already receives `package_name` as a parameter, calls `triple_to_role(triple)` (crates/callisto-graph/src/napi.rs) to destructure `(platform, arch, abi)` and passes those values -- not the raw triple string -- into this function when constructing `PlatformTarget.artifact_name`."
    },
    {
      "name": "callisto-action native artifact placement loop",
      "signature": "New shell logic inside `.github/actions/callisto-action/action.yml`'s existing `callisto-orchestrate` composite step, placed inside the `[[ -n \"$INPUT_PUBLISH\" && \"$INPUT_PUBLISH\" != \"false\" ]]` guard (action.yml:167) immediately after `NATIVE_MATRIX` is computed (action.yml:164-165) and before `callisto plan-publish`/publish executes.",
      "description": "Feeds `NATIVE_MATRIX` through process substitution, not a pipe -- `while IFS= read -r ENTRY; do ... done < <(printf '%s' \"$NATIVE_MATRIX\" | jq -c '.[]')` -- matching the existing pattern used for the releases loop at action.yml:219, so an `exit 1` inside the loop body terminates the step itself, not a subshell. For each ENTRY: extracts `ARTIFACT=$(echo \"$ENTRY\" | jq -r '.artifactName')` and `DIR_RAW=$(echo \"$ENTRY\" | jq -r '.packageDir')`; sets `DIR` to `.` when `DIR_RAW` is the empty string (the real value `packageDir` takes for a workspace-root package, per crates/callisto-graph/src/commands/matrix.rs:34-39), otherwise `DIR=$DIR_RAW`; runs `mkdir -p \"$DIR\"` explicitly; then runs `gh run download \"$GITHUB_RUN_ID\" --name \"$ARTIFACT\" --dir \"$DIR\" || { echo \"::error::failed to download artifact '$ARTIFACT' for placement into '$DIR' within run $GITHUB_RUN_ID\"; exit 1; }`. Runs once per array element, in array order. Skipped entirely (loop body never executes) when `NATIVE_MATRIX` is `[]`."
    }
  ],
  "acceptance_criteria": [
    {
      "id": "AC-001",
      "criterion": "Given two distinct packages in the same workspace that each declare the same platform target triple (e.g. both declare aarch64-apple-darwin), `callisto matrix --format json`'s output contains two distinct entries for that triple -- one per package -- and their `artifactName` values differ, each shaped `<that package's packageName>-<platform>-<arch>` (or `<packageName>-<platform>-<arch>-<abi>` when the triple carries an abi, e.g. a musl or gnu linux triple), where `<platform>`/`<arch>`/`<abi>` are the same platform/arch/abi values that entry's own `platform`/`arch`/`abi` fields report (derived from the triple, not the triple string itself). For example, packages `pkg-a` and `pkg-b` both declaring `aarch64-apple-darwin` produce `artifactName` values `pkg-a-darwin-arm64` and `pkg-b-darwin-arm64` -- distinct because `packageName` differs, containing no raw triple substring, no vendor component (e.g. `apple`, `pc`, `unknown`), and no fixed `native-` prefix.",
      "is_error_case": false
    },
    {
      "id": "AC-002",
      "criterion": "Given the same two-packages-one-triple scenario as AC-001, `.github/actions/callisto-action/action.yml`'s `NATIVE_MATRIX` computation (which pipes callisto matrix's output through `unique_by(.artifactName)`) retains both packages' entries rather than collapsing them to one, because their artifactName values are now distinct.",
      "is_error_case": false
    },
    {
      "id": "AC-003",
      "criterion": "Given a workflow run where N per-platform build jobs each upload an artifact via actions/upload-artifact using the exact artifactName string from that platform's callisto matrix --format json entry, and the callisto-orchestrate step's placement loop (placed inside the `[[ -n \"$INPUT_PUBLISH\" && \"$INPUT_PUBLISH\" != \"false\" ]]` guard, not merely somewhere after NATIVE_MATRIX computation) executes: after the step completes, each of the N artifacts' downloaded file contents are present under the corresponding packageDir path relative to the working directory (with the directory having been created via the loop's own mkdir -p if it did not already exist), and this placement completes before the step's callisto plan-publish/publish invocation begins.",
      "is_error_case": false
    },
    {
      "id": "AC-004",
      "criterion": "Given nativeMatrix is [] (no native platform-target packages in the workspace) and INPUT_PUBLISH is non-empty and not the literal string \"false\" (so the guard at action.yml:167 is entered), the placement loop body never executes (zero gh run download invocations) because NATIVE_MATRIX's parsed array is empty, and the step proceeds directly to callisto plan-publish/publish exactly as it did before this change. Given INPUT_PUBLISH is unset or the literal string \"false\", the placement loop is never reached regardless of nativeMatrix's contents, because the loop is placed inside that same guard.",
      "is_error_case": false
    },
    {
      "id": "AC-005",
      "criterion": "Given a nativeMatrix entry whose packageDir field is the empty string (the value produced for a workspace-root package whose manifest has no parent directory component), the placement loop sets DIR to \".\" before calling mkdir -p and gh run download --dir, and the corresponding artifact's contents are placed directly under the working directory rather than the step failing or attempting mkdir -p with an empty path.",
      "is_error_case": false
    },
    {
      "id": "AC-006",
      "criterion": "Given a nativeMatrix entry whose artifactName was never uploaded within the current workflow run ($GITHUB_RUN_ID) -- including the case where an artifact with that exact name exists in a different, older, or unrelated workflow run in the same repository -- the loop's `gh run download \"$GITHUB_RUN_ID\" --name \"$ARTIFACT\" --dir \"$DIR\"` invocation exits non-zero because the run-id pins the lookup to the current run only, the `|| { ...; exit 1; }` clause terminates the step (not merely a pipeline subshell) before reaching callisto plan-publish/publish, and the step's log output names the specific missing artifactName and the DIR value (post `.`-normalization where applicable) that download attempt was targeting.",
      "is_error_case": true
    },
    {
      "id": "AC-007",
      "criterion": "ARCHITECTURE.md §12's example workflow code block (the fenced code block following the \"Zero-Config GitHub Actions Workflow Pattern\" heading) contains no occurrence of the string \"callisto publish-target\" anywhere within it; the status note above the heading (naming Java/JNI, .NET native-AOT, and the callisto publish-target subcommand as unimplemented) is unchanged and still present.",
      "is_error_case": false
    },
    {
      "id": "AC-008",
      "criterion": "ARCHITECTURE.md §12's example workflow's build job uses the namespaced GitHub Actions matrix form matching callisto-matrix.md §6.1's own documented consumption pattern: `strategy.matrix.target` is set to `${{ fromJson(needs.matrix.outputs.nativeMatrix) }}`, and every per-entry field is dereferenced under the `matrix.target.` prefix -- `runs-on: ${{ matrix.target.hostRunner }}`, plus `matrix.target.triple`, `matrix.target.artifactName`, and `matrix.target.packageDir` in the build/upload steps -- with each of these four resolving to a real field on PlatformTarget's camelCase JSON serialization (hostRunner, triple, artifactName, packageDir). The block contains no bare, non-namespaced `matrix.os` or `matrix.package` reference (neither is a real field under any prefix), and contains no use of `matrix.target` as a plain scalar value (e.g. `${{ matrix.target }}` alone) -- `matrix.target` appears only as the namespace prefix for a specific field.",
      "is_error_case": false
    },
    {
      "id": "AC-009",
      "criterion": "ARCHITECTURE.md §12's example workflow code block contains all of: (a) a build job's `actions/upload-artifact` step with `name: ${{ matrix.target.artifactName }}`; (b) a release job step invoking `./.github/actions/callisto-action` with `publish: true`; (c) a comment block immediately preceding that callisto-action step containing both the substring \"uploaded artifact into its packageDir\" and the substring \"automatically inside this step\" -- documenting that placement happens automatically within the action, not via a separate consumer-authored step. The block contains no consumer-authored `gh run download` invocation or other literal placement step, consistent with AC-010 and AC-011.",
      "is_error_case": false
    },
    {
      "id": "AC-010",
      "criterion": "A consumer workflow that already uses callisto-action for publishing and adds only a matrix-driven build job (per .claude/specs/callisto-matrix.md §6.1's nativeMatrix consumption pattern) uploading artifacts named per artifactName, with no additional bash, composite action, or script authored by the consumer to perform placement, ends with the callisto-orchestrate step having invoked gh run download once per nativeMatrix entry and each entry's artifact contents present at that entry's packageDir (post `.`-normalization) -- with no requirement on this spec's part that callisto publish itself subsequently succeeds, per this spec's non-goal on the publish-side packaging round-trip.",
      "is_error_case": false
    },
    {
      "id": "AC-011",
      "criterion": ".claude/specs/callisto-matrix.md §6.2's standalone reference snippet (for consumers building their own custom multi-job pipeline outside callisto-action, where nativeMatrix genuinely crosses a `needs.<job>.outputs.nativeMatrix` job-output boundary) is updated to pin the download to `$GITHUB_RUN_ID` and to `mkdir -p \"$DIR\"` before calling `gh run download`, and §6.2 gains an explicit note stating that consumers using callisto-action for publishing do not need to write this snippet themselves -- callisto-action performs equivalent placement automatically, in-step, once nativeMatrix is non-empty and publish is enabled.",
      "is_error_case": false
    }
  ],
  "linked_requirement": "REQ-001",
  "revision_note": "Three corrections across two passes. (1) AC-008, corrected per lambda:exit-gate's contradiction report and already verified accurate by canon:gate-spec -- unchanged in this round. Original AC-008 required ARCHITECTURE.md §12's example to use a bare `${{ matrix.hostRunner }}` form and forbade any `matrix.target` reference. Direct inspection of the committed ARCHITECTURE.md (lines 486-501) shows the implemented example correctly uses `strategy: matrix: target: ${{ fromJson(...) }}` with namespaced `matrix.target.<field>` dereferences (hostRunner, triple, artifactName, packageDir) -- the only valid GitHub Actions form for that strategy shape, and the exact pattern already published as canonical in .claude/specs/callisto-matrix.md §6.1 lines 287-289 (the same pattern AC-010 itself cites). AC-008 was rewritten to require the namespaced `matrix.target.<field>` form and forbid only the genuinely nonexistent bare `matrix.os`/`matrix.package` fields and bare-scalar `matrix.target` use. (2) AC-009, corrected per canon:gate-spec's FAIL blocker on this AC-008-corrected spec. Original AC-009 required the example to, 'read end to end, show... a placement step downloading that artifact into its package directory' -- an untestable human-reading judgment with no mechanical predicate (unlike sibling AC-007/AC-008's greppable string conditions), and one that contradicted AC-010 (no consumer-authored placement script) and AC-011 (callisto-action performs placement automatically in-step): a literal reading of AC-009 would require adding a `gh run download` step to §12's example, violating the pattern AC-010/AC-011 establish. Direct inspection of ARCHITECTURE.md lines 474-513 confirms the example represents placement only via a three-line comment (lines 507-509: 'Placement of each build job's uploaded artifact into its packageDir, / then callisto publish, both happen automatically inside this step / once nativeMatrix is non-empty and publish is enabled.') immediately preceding the `./.github/actions/callisto-action` step with `publish: true`; there is no literal placement step anywhere in the block. AC-009 was rewritten to require exactly this observed structure -- the `actions/upload-artifact` step's `name: ${{ matrix.target.artifactName }}`, the callisto-action invocation with `publish: true`, and a preceding comment containing the two exact observed substrings -- and to explicitly forbid any consumer-authored `gh run download` step, making AC-009 consistent with AC-010 and AC-011 rather than contradicting them. Checked all other criteria for dependency on either claim: none reference matrix field forms or the placement-step-presence question, so no further criteria required revision. AC-008 and AC-009 left byte-identical in this round per instruction. (3) AC-001/scope/api_surface/non_goals[17], corrected in this round after direct inspection of the committed crates/callisto-graph/src/matrix.rs (lines 36-52, 138-155) contradicted the spec's prior claim of an `artifact_name_for_package_triple(package_name, triple) -> \"native-{package_name}-{triple}\"` function. The function actually implemented and tested (see `artifact_name_for_package_platform_embeds_package_name_and_platform_arch_abi` and `build_platform_target_combines_role_and_ci_table` in matrix.rs's own test module) is `artifact_name_for_package_platform(package_name: &str, platform: &str, arch: &str, abi: Option<&str>) -> String`, returning `{package_name}-{platform}-{arch}` (or with `-{abi}` appended when `abi` is `Some`) -- e.g. `native-mod-darwin-arm64`, `other-pkg-linux-x64-musl`. `build_platform_target` calls `triple_to_role` (crates/callisto-graph/src/napi.rs:136-163, proven bijective across all 18 known triples by the existing `triple_to_role_known_triples_round_trip` test) to destructure the triple into `(platform, arch, abi)` before calling the naming function, rather than passing the raw triple through. This is napi-rs's own documented published-platform-package and recommended-CI-artifact-name convention (https://napi.rs/docs/deep-dive/release: `<name>-<platform>-<arch>[-<abi>]`, e.g. `@scope/addon-darwin-arm64`, `@scope/addon-win32-x64-msvc`) rather than the raw-triple-with-vendor-component convention (e.g. cargo-dist's `ripgrep-15.2.0-aarch64-apple-darwin.tar.gz`), which belongs to a different, already-excluded (Tier 2 / GitHub Release asset) distribution model. scope's Rust-change sentence, the api_surface entry (renamed from `artifact_name_for_package_triple` to `artifact_name_for_package_platform` with the real signature), AC-001 (rewritten with a concrete falsifiable example matching the real format), and non_goals[17] (reworded to name both stale formats it supersedes -- the original raw-triple format and the never-implemented triple-shaped intermediate format -- while keeping the 'artifactName is an opaque CI-internal correlation token, not a versioned contract' point and the no-config-override rationale) were corrected accordingly. AC-002 through AC-011 (excluding AC-008/AC-009, untouched per instruction) were checked for references to the stale function name or triple-shaped format; none contain any -- AC-002's `unique_by(.artifactName)` dedup logic is format-agnostic and holds unchanged under the corrected format, so no further criteria required revision.",
  "reasoning": "Verified this round's contradiction directly against live source: read crates/callisto-graph/src/matrix.rs in full (lines 1-155 and the test module at 472-1062), confirming `artifact_name_for_package_platform`'s real signature, its two format arms, its call site inside `build_platform_target` (which passes `triple_to_role`-destructured platform/arch/abi, not the raw triple), and two passing tests that pin the exact output strings (`native-mod-darwin-arm64`, `other-pkg-linux-x64-musl`, and the `pkg-a-darwin-arm64`/`pkg-b-darwin-arm64` pair in `build_matrix_report_same_triple_two_packages_have_distinct_artifact_names`). Also confirmed via the module's `triple_host_runner_use_cross` and `triple_to_role` tables that all 18 known triples map to pairwise-distinct (platform, arch, abi) tuples, so the collision-safety property AC-001/AC-002 rely on continues to hold under the corrected format for the same structural reason as before -- package_name uniqueness -- not because of anything specific to the platform/arch/abi decomposition itself. Chose the napi-rs platform-arch-abi convention framing for the revision_note and non_goals rewrite because it is independently verifiable from napi-rs's own public documentation and directly explains why the implemented format looks the way it does, rather than treating it as an arbitrary implementation deviation. Prior round's AC-008/AC-009 revision history preserved verbatim in revision_note per the skill's cumulative-revision-note pattern; those two criteria's own text was not touched."
}

